@Antonialock @ValWood
I think this a good example of how difficult it is to trace erroneous ARBA annotations. 
Would you be ok discussing this in an ARBA discussion for the IEA working group?

there is a lot to respond to here....I'll try to get started

@ValWood for ARBA00089174 the annotation is not applied to cerevisiae since cerevisiae doesn't hit those two funfams. 
there is only one human entry with this FUNFAM combination , some species have two, e.g. dicty and zebrafish. Pombe has a NAS - if this had been a TAS the annotation would have been taken into account - I think you can TAS from this paper: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6333459/

_"Cps1 (carboxypeptidase S) is a vacuolar carboxypeptidase that is predicted to belong to a family of zinc metalloproteases [[122](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6333459/#CIT0122),[123](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6333459/#CIT0123)]. Although its intracellular function remains unclear, Cps1 is likely to participate in hydrolysis of leucine from the C terminus of proteins along with Prc1, as Cps1 is required for growth in Prc1-deficient strains when a synthetic dipeptide with leucine as the C-terminal amino acid is provided as the sole source of nitrogen [[124](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6333459/#CIT0124),[125](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6333459/#CIT0125)]."_

for "ARBA rules" I think this is what you are asking for:
Minimum support refers to the number of Swiss-Prot templates that are required for rule generation. Currently, at least 5 Swiss-Prot templates with a specific annotation and consistent signatures should exist to create a rule with InterPro and/or Panther signatures; and at least 2 - for FunFam signatures (since analysis has shown that FunFam signatures seem to be more reliable and conservative). Minimum support is a configurable setting and can be adjusted manually.
